1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Dan: Alright, thanks everyone for hopping on, let's get started with today's refinement session.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Priya: Sounds good, I have about six items lined up for us to get through today.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Dan: Okay, PROJ dash four twelve, so the export thing again. The nightly export times out for our largest customers whenever a report crosses fifty thousand rows, and support has escalated it three times this month, so we really need to fix this one properly this quarter.

4
00:01:20,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Priya: Got it. I think the acceptance bar here is that the export has to complete for a fifty thousand row report without timing out, since that is the number support keeps citing back to us.

5
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Dan: Also, this can't move forward until proj three ninety eight lands, since that data migration is a hard blocker on the export path.

6
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Priya: Understood, I will go ping the migration team and see where that one actually stands this week.

7
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Dan: One more thing, we also don't have a ticket yet for the retry-backoff work on the export job, so somebody should file one for that after this call.

8
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Priya: Next, PROJ 413, this is the login flow ticket. The SSO redirect keeps dropping query params whenever a client uses their own identity provider, so folks land on the dashboard instead of wherever they were actually headed.

9
00:04:45,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Dan: Right, and the acceptance criterion has to be that a redirect preserves the original query string byte for byte, since that is exactly what broke on us last time.

10
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Priya: Also flagging that this one already has a description in Jira from Marcus, so whoever applies this later should be careful not to clobber his existing notes.

11
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Dan: Okay, platform seventy seven, the auth-service migration. We need to cut the staging environment over before next month's audit, and QA wants a full regression pass on top of that.

12
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Priya: Makes sense. I do not think we actually settled on a hard deadline for the cutover itself, so that one is still open.

13
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Dan: Okay, back to platform seventy seven for one second, quick follow-up on the auth cutover timeline before we move on to the next item.

14
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Priya: Right, and to close that out, the follow-up is just that security still needs to sign off before the cutover can be scheduled at all.

15
00:08:00,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Dan: Moving on to PROJ four fifty five, the reporting pipeline overhaul. This is the big one, so let's take our time on it, since a lot of teams depend on this pipeline every single night.

16
00:10:00,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Priya: Right, the core problem is that the aggregation step reprocesses the entire history on every run instead of just the new rows, which is why nightly runs keep creeping past their window.

17
00:12:30,000 --> 00:14:59,000
Dan: So the acceptance criterion I would propose is that a nightly run only reprocesses rows added since the previous successful run, and finishes inside the existing two hour window.

18
00:15:00,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Priya: Agreed, and out of scope for this one is backfilling historical data, that is a separate effort we already have a spike ticket open for.

19
00:17:45,000 --> 00:19:59,000
Dan: One open question here is whether we snapshot the aggregation state hourly or daily, and nobody on this call actually has a strong opinion either way yet.

20
00:20:00,000 --> 00:21:59,000
Priya: Fair, let's leave that one open for now and come back to it once we have looked at the storage cost either way.

21
00:22:15,000 --> 00:23:59,000
Dan: One last thing on this ticket, the goal here is that large customers stop filing export tickets altogether once the pipeline is reliable.

22
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:20,000
Priya: Great, I think that covers everything for today, thanks all.
